  "contents": "Is this clause enforceable in a residential lease in Ontario? Cite the section.\n\nRead the clause as a term of an ordinary private residential tenancy in Ontario that the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 covers: the rental unit has no exemption under section 6.1, and it is not student housing, a care home, a co-operative or a mobile home park.\n\nClause: To carry out repairs, the Landlord may enter the unit on 24 hours' written notice stating the reason and the day, at any time between 7 a.m. and 10 p.m.\n\nEnd your answer with these two lines:\nVerdict: enforceable (or: Verdict: not enforceable)\nSection: <the number of the one section of the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 that decides it>\n",
  "config": {
   "max_output_tokens": 16000
  }
 },
 "requested_at": "2026-11-13T20:09:33.679588Z",
 "text": "No, this clause is not enforceable. \n\nUnder **section 27** of the *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006* (specifically subsection 27(3)), when a landlord gives 24 hours' written notice to enter a rental unit to carry out repairs, the written notice must specify:\n1. The reason for entry;\n2. The day of entry; and\n3. **A time of entry between the hours of 8 a.m. and 8 p.m.**\n\nThe lease clause violates section 27 in two ways:\n* **Unlawful hours:** It purports to allow entry as early as 7 a.m. and as late as 10 p.m., outside the statutory window of 8 a.m. to 8 p.m.\n* **Missing time of entry:** It removes the requirement that the notice specify a time (or reasonable window of time) for the entry, purporting instead to allow entry \"at any time\" on the stated day.\n\nBecause a landlord may only enter a unit in accordance with the Act (s. 25) and any lease term inconsistent with the Act is void (s. 4), the clause cannot be enforced.\n\nVerdict: not enforceable\nSection: 27",
 "finish_reason": "STOP",
